134
00:10:07.986 --> 00:10:12.811
All right I want to describe some general

135
00:10:12.811 --> 00:10:18.217
things about the overall
program development process or

136
00:10:18.217 --> 00:10:25.226
cycle when you want to write programs
in the Hack machine language.

137
00:10:25.226 --> 00:10:28.822
First of all,
your programs are going to reside

138
00:10:28.822 --> 00:10:34.200
within regular text files that you
can write with any text editor.

139
00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:37.550
So, you invoke your favorite text editor.

140
00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:38.540
You write the program.

141
00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:42.410
You save it using the extension asm.

142
00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:48.510
And by convention we give our programs
names that begin with capital letters so

143
00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.210
we give it a name, .asm.

144
00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:57.080
And then you load this program
as is into the CPU Emulator.

145
00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:00.930
As I explained before, the CPU Emulator
has this very nice service.

146
00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:04.656
That when you load the program it
automatically translates the program into

147
00:11:04.656 --> 00:11:06.732
binary code.

148
00:11:06.732 --> 00:11:11.770
So you can next run the program
on the CPU Emulator and

149
00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:15.220
ask yourself if you're
satisfied with the results.

150
00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:17.070
If so, you are done.

151
00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:20.320
You can submit the program to us and

152
00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:24.990
if you are unsatisfied with the program
which will be a probably the case in

153
00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:29.360
the first few iterations of the debugging,
then you will, you fix the program.

154
00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:34.620
You look for errors and you fix
the errors and you fix them by going back

155
00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:39.710
into the text editor, you know, working
in the editor, saving the program again.

156
00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:42.650
Reloading it into the CPU Emulator,
and so on.

157
00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:46.160
So it's quite convenient to
have two windows open on the,

158
00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:51.290
on your screen at the same time, the text
editor here, CPU Emulator there, and

159
00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:55.550
you can easily, you know,
move between the two but

160
00:11:55.550 --> 00:12:00.350
don't forget to load your updated
program after you you fix it.

161
00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:08.500
The error diagnostics of
the CPU Emulator is quite primitive.

162
00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:14.840
So when you load the program into
the CPU Emulator, if there's a syntax

163
00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:19.980
error in this program, the CPU Emulator
will simply refuse to load it.

164
00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:25.474
It will issue some cryptic error
message something like oh,

165
00:12:25.474 --> 00:12:30.394
it will give you some very important
information and to give you the line

166
00:12:30.394 --> 00:12:34.752
number with error occurred and
the first error that was found.

167
00:12:34.752 --> 00:12:38.243
And then you can go into this line
number and and fix the problem.

168
00:12:38.243 --> 00:12:42.826
And once you, you are done with the,
with the syntax e, errors,

169
00:12:42.826 --> 00:12:46.034
well, then the real problems begin,
you know?

170
00:12:46.034 --> 00:12:48.842
These are the run-time errors,
which are much harder to detect.

171
00:12:48.842 --> 00:12:54.162
And you typically detect them by
watching the execution of your program,

172
00:12:54.162 --> 00:12:57.214
seeing that something
does not work properly.

173
00:12:57.214 --> 00:13:00.093
And then once again you have
to go through the same cycle.

174
00:13:00.093 --> 00:13:03.912
And fix your program until it
works to your satisfaction.

175
00:13:03.912 --> 00:13:08.985
So this is the general mechanics
of developing a program

176
00:13:08.985 --> 00:13:12.296
using a CPU Emulator and a text editor.

177
00:13:12.296 --> 00:13:19.679
And as you see it, it's quite,
it's quite simple and friendly.

178
00:13:19.679 --> 00:13:24.433
All right finally I want to say
a few words about best practice and

179
00:13:24.433 --> 00:13:27.327
advice on do's and do, and don'ts.

180
00:13:27.327 --> 00:13:32.231
There's no reason really
to think about a machine

181
00:13:32.231 --> 00:13:37.255
language programs as some sort of a,
a strange animal.

182
00:13:37.255 --> 00:13:43.650
It's yet another example of a,
a programming art effect.

183
00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:47.890
And all the principles that
applied to writing programs

184
00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:51.410
in a high-level language
also apply at the low level.

185
00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:57.420
We expect your programs to be short,
or not unnecessarily long.

186
00:13:57.420 --> 00:14:02.270
They should be short, they should be
efficient, they should be elegant, and

187
00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:04.770
they should be self-describing.

188
00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:11.820
So we expect you to document the programs,
but do it by using some judgment.

189
00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:14.700
You know, don't over-document the program.

190
00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:19.950
The best, I think thing to do is
simply to look at our examples and

191
00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:23.760
to follow something similar,
so, you know, typically

192
00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:30.540
we use some high-level
operation to describe you know?

193
00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:35.280
We start a comment, we write something
like, here we're going to say,

194
00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:39.320
if i is greater than n, goto,
and then, we write the 6 or

195
00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:45.350
7 instructions in machine code that
actually implement this this semantics.

196
00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:53.390
Now here's some very important technical
tips that will make your life easier.

197
00:14:53.390 --> 00:14:59.270
First of all, you must use
symbolic variables and labels.

198
00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:02.900
You know, otherwise your
programs will be helplessly and

199
00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:07.140
hopelessly complex, and and cryptic.

200
00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:12.360
So, if you want to go to somewhere
in your program, use a label.

201
00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:16.536
If you want to store something
repetitively, make up a variable name.

202
00:15:16.536 --> 00:15:20.640
So, when you're done writing the program,
you know, look at it,

203
00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:25.030
and make sure that you don't see any
actual educes there, you have to make

204
00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:29.340
sure that everything is symbolic, you
know, this is one very important ritual,

205
00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:34.810
of well-written machine language,
language programs.

206
00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:36.660
When you invent your variables and

207
00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:42.030
your labels, as usual in programming,
use sensible names.

208
00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:49.260
You know, don't make cryptic names like
gu5 3 percent or something like this.

209
00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:55.723
Use instead something like loop end,
stop, positive, negative.

210
00:15:55.723 --> 00:15:58.793
You know, depending on what
is it that you want to do,

211
00:15:58.793 --> 00:16:02.910
try to use sensible label names and,
likewise, variable names.

212
00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:08.713
You know, use nice names, like i,
end, some, count, and, and so on.

213
00:16:08.713 --> 00:16:15.356
Now, when you declare or, or
when you use these variable names,

214
00:16:15.356 --> 00:16:21.300
use lower-case for variables and
upper-case for labels.

215
00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:24.940
If you go back to our program examples,
you will see that all the labels,

216
00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:29.090
you know typically words like END and
STOP were uppercase, and

217
00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:34.250
all the variables i, n, x,
y and so on were lower case.

218
00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:37.910
If you follow this convention, which
is not required by the way, you know,

219
00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:41.730
it's, it's not something that they,
that the assembler cares about.

220
00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:45.735
But if you use this convention you
will have a very easy time for

221
00:16:45.735 --> 00:16:49.440
yourself to distinguish
between variables and labels.

222
00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:53.810
So when you see an @ command when
an A command, when you see something about

223
00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:57.287
@sum, if the sum is lower-case,
